          %h1#the_meaning_explanations_of_type_theory The meaning explanations of type theory
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#natural_numbers"} Natural numbers
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#function_types"} Function types
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#identity_types"} Identity types
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#other_judgements"} Other judgements
                %ul
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#type_equality"} Type equality
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#hypothetical_judgements"} Hypothetical judgements
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#the_rules_of_type_theory"} The rules of type theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-L%C3%B6f"}> Per Martin-Löf
            ‘s
            %strong meaning explanations of type theory
            provide a way to justify the correctness of the rules of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgements"} judgements
            of type theory are defined by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation"} computation
            of closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+form"} canonical form
            in some untyped notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation"} computation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lambda+calculus"} lambda calculus
            extended with constants for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+introduction"} constructors
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+elimination"} eliminators
            of type theory.
          %p
            From a technical/mathematical point of view, the meaning explanations give rise to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/realizability+model"} realizability model
            of type theory, where realizers are terms in some untyped notion of computation, such as an extended lambda calculus. That is, it can be regarded as a description of how types are assigned to untyped computations. However, the term “meaning explanations” also refer to the “philosophical” or “pre-mathematical” component, which regards this as an “explanation” of the “constructive validity” of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgements"} judgements
            of the type theory.
          %p
            The standard meaning explanations validate not just the rules of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
            but also the stronger rules of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional type theory
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
          %p Martin-Löf has been quoted as saying
          %blockquote
            %p Some people feel that when I have presented my meaning explanations I have said nothing. And this is how it should be. The standard semantics should be just that: standard. It should come as no surprise.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Suppose we have given some collection of computational objects which we call “terms”, with an
            %em untyped
            notion of “reduction” or “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation"}> computation
            ”. These could be terms in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lambda+calculus"} lambda calculus
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+combinatory+algebra"} partial combinatory algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\Rightarrow y
            to mean that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            computes or reduces to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            (in some number of steps). We then define simultaneously the following
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicates"} predicates
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b,A
            are
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a type, often written
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\;type
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              has type
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              are equal elements of type
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b:A
          %p
            The definition is by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction-recursion"} induction-recursion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a type”
            = succeed "," do
              %em inductively
            %em recursively
            two functions on such
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p The definition is intended to satisfy some properties:
          %ul
            %li
              %p Equality:
              %ul
                %li
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
                  if and only if
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo =
                          %mi a
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=a:A
                %li
                  If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo =
                        %mi b
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b:A
                  then
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi b
                          %mo =
                          %mi a
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b=a:A
                %li
                  If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo =
                        %mi b
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b:A
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mi c
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b=c:A
                  then
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo =
                          %mi c
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=c:A
            %li
              %p Reverse reduction:
              %ul
                %li
                  If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi type
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\;type
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \Rightarrow V
                  then
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi type
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\;type
                %li The other judgment forms similarly respect reverse reduction in all positions.
          %p
            By reflexivity, the specification of which terms have a type is technically redundant. It is just reiterating the reflexivity instances of equality. So for this meaning explanation, the recursive part of the definition is assigning types their meaning as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+equivalence+relations"} partial equivalence relations
            (PERs) on terms.
          %p
            We expect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
            %em if and only if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=a:A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b:A
            implies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=a:A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b=b:A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b:A
          %p
            The reverse reduction properties are saying that the relevant information about a term comes from the value it reduces to. Meaning explanations provide a sense in which type theory is about computational behavior. For this system, the computational behavior of a term is to return a value. Note however that not all terms reduce to a value, for example, the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            combinator:
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi x
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi x
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x.x\,x) (\lambda x.x\,x)
          %p The clauses we should include in the inductive-recursive definition depend on what type constructors we want to put into our type theory; in each case the base notion of untyped computation must include corresponding operators. Here are three paradigmatic examples.
          %h3#natural_numbers Natural numbers
          %p
            Suppose that we have terms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(x)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursion"} recursor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi rec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi r
                  %mo .
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} rec(n,z_0,x\,r. z_s)
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mn 0
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi v
                    %mrow
                      %mi rec
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi r
                      %mo .
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{n \Rightarrow 0 \qquad z_0 \Rightarrow v}
                  {rec(n,z_0,x\,r.z_s) \Rightarrow v}
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi rec
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi r
                      %mo .
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi r
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi v
                    %mrow
                      %mi rec
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi r
                      %mo .
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi v
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{n' \Rightarrow s(n) \qquad z_s[n/x,rec(n,z_0,x\,r.z_s)/r] \Rightarrow v}
                  {rec(n',z_0,x\,r. z_s) \Rightarrow v}.
          %p
            The term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi rec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi r
                  %mo .
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} rec(n,z_0,x\,r. z_s)
            is intended to denote the value at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            of a recursively defined function specified to be
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_0
            at zero and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_s
            at successors. Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            which may occur free in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_s
            but are bound in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi rec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi r
                    %mo .
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} rec(n,z_0,x\,r. z_s)
          %p
            Then to explain the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+type"} natural numbers type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+definition"} inductive definition
            of “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a type” which says
          %ul
            %li
              if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\Rightarrow N
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a type.
          %p The corresponding clause for elements is
          %ul
            %li
              if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\Rightarrow N
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
              is defined inductively by the clauses:
              %ul
                %li
                  if
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\Rightarrow 0
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
                %li
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\Rightarrow s(b)
                  and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi b
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b:A
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
          %p
            Note that the recurrence in this inductive definition doesn’t go via the overall recursive definition of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
          %ul
            %li
              an inductive predicate
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi IsNat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} IsNat
              %ul
                %li
                  if
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\Rightarrow 0
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi IsNat
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} IsNat(a)
                %li
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\Rightarrow s(b)
                  and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi IsNat
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} IsNat(b)
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi IsNat
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} IsNat(a)
          %p Then say
          %ul
            %li
              if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\Rightarrow N
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
              is those such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi IsNat
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} IsNat(a)
          %p The clause for equality is
          %ul
            %li
              if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\Rightarrow N
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b:A
              is defined inductively by the clauses:
              %ul
                %li
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\Rightarrow 0
                  and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi b
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\Rightarrow 0
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo =
                          %mi b
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b:A
                %li
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\Rightarrow s(a')
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi b
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\Rightarrow s(b')
                  and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo ′
                          %mo =
                          %mi b
                          %mo ′
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a'=b':A
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo =
                          %mi b
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b:A
          %p With a similar separation of the inductive definition.
          %h3#function_types Function types
          %p
            Suppose that we have a term constructor yielding “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to B
            ” for terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi app
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} app(f,a)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda x.t
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi λ
                      %mi x
                      %mo .
                      %mi t
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi v
                    %mrow
                      %mi app
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi v
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{f \Rightarrow \lambda x.t \qquad t[a/x] \Rightarrow v}
                  {app(f, a) \Rightarrow v}.
          %p
            Then to explain the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a type” which says
          %ul
            %li
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a type and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              is a type and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\Rightarrow (A\to B)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a type.
          %p The clause for elements is:
          %ul
            %li
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\Rightarrow (A\to B)
              for types
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=a':A
              we have
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi app
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi app
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} app(f,a)=app(f,a'):B
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to B
              is the collection of terms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              such that for any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=a':A
              we have
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi app
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi app
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} app(f,a)=app(f,a'):B
          %p For equality, we have:
          %ul
            %li
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\Rightarrow (A\to B)
              for types
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=a':A
              we have
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi app
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi app
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} app(f,a)=app(g,a'):B
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo =
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f=g:C
          %p
            These are not inductive definitions as we had for the natural numbers; they are merely simple definitions. On the other hand, we are now using the fact that the function mapping types to their collections of element pairs is recursively defined, since in order to make this definition, we must invoke it at
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=a':A
            in this clause (and similar occurrences in other clauses for higher order types) is the reason why the whole explanation cannot just be defined by mutual induction.
          %p
            This clause for function types is not the one Martin-Löf proposed. Unlike all of Martin-Löf’s clauses, this one gives
            %em all
            terms of a function type directly; it doesn’t just give the canonical terms first, with the rest defined via reduction. This is because it defines the elements in reference to the elimination form,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi app
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} app
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
          %ul
            %li
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\Rightarrow (A\to B)
              for types
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \Rightarrow \lambda x.b
              for some (variable
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and term)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=a':A
              we have
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b[a/x]=b[a'/x]:B
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:C
          %p The element equality clause gets an analogous change. (Exercise!)
          %p Both styles of function type validate all the usual function rules of extensional type theory.
          %h3#identity_types Identity types
          %p
            Suppose we have a term constructor yielding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_A(a,b)
            for terms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,a,b
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi refl
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} refl
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a type” the clause
          %ul
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a type,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b:A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ⇒
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \Rightarrow Id_A(a,b)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a type.
          %p
            Here we are finally using the fact that typehood and elements are defined by induction-recursion: this clause in the
            %em inductive
            definition of typehood refers to the value of the
            %em recursive
            elementhood function at some smaller type.
          %p The clause for elements is:
          %ul
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a type,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b:A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ⇒
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \Rightarrow Id_A(a,b)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:C
              just if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b:A
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi refl
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \Rightarrow refl
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Id
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Id_A(a,b)
              contains something only if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b:A
          %p The clause for equality is:
          %ul
            %li
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a type and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b:A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo ⇒
                      %msub
                        %mi Id
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \Rightarrow Id_A(a,b)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %mi q
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p=q:C
              just if
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b:A
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi refl
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\Rightarrow refl
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi refl
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q\Rightarrow refl
          %p
            Because we have deliberately only put
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi refl
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} refl
            (and terms reducing to it) into the identity type, this definition of identity types will validate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+UIP"} uniqueness of identity proofs
            (UIP). Moreover, because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_A(a,b)
            is inhabited if and only if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b:A
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional type theory
          %p
            There are simple ways to modify the meaning explanation so as to no longer validate the extensional rules. For instance, if we add to the semantics additional “free variables”, so that there are “neutral terms” which do not compute to any canonical form, and modify the rules of the interpretation so as to say that any normalizable neutral term inhabits every type, then extensionality will no longer hold, since the identity type (like any type) will contain all the variables. This is maybe not a particularly compelling model of intensionality, but there are proposals for better ways to expand the meaning explanation to deal with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
          %h3#other_judgements Other judgements
          %h4#type_equality Type equality
          %p The easy way is to say that
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A=B
              means that
              %ul
                %li
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi type
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\;type
                  and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi B
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi type
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\;type
                %li
                  for all
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
                  if and only if
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo :
                          %mi B
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:B
                %li
                  for all
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                  and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi b
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo =
                        %mi b
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b:A
                  if and only if
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo =
                          %mi b
                          %mo :
                          %mi B
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b:B
          %p
            This validates the conversion rules. It’s extensional in a manner similar to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nuprl"} Nuprl
            uses a more intensional type equality, inductively defining
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A=B
            along with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\;type
          %h4#hypothetical_judgements Hypothetical judgements
          %p Hypothetical judgments are explained by induction on the length of the context. The base case is exhausted by the explanations of categorical judgment, so it is enough to focus our attention on the inductive step.
          %p
            Suppose that we already know what a hypothetical judgment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash J
            means for any judgment
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma, x : A \vdash J
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash J [M/x]
            for any
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi M
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash M : A
            plus an additional conditions demanding that definitional equality be preserved: for example, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b : B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi M
                    %mo =
                    %mi M
                    %mo ′
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash M = M' : A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi M
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash b [M/x] = b [M'/x] : B [M/x]
          %h3#the_rules_of_type_theory The rules of type theory
          %p
            The claim is that by inspecting these definitions, one can arrive at the formal rules of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            for manipulating judgments such as “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a type”, “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A
            ”, and “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b:A
            ”, which no longer refer to any given notion of computation. However, Martin-Löf famously wrote
          %blockquote
            %p …there are … certain limits to what verbal explanations can do when it comes to justifying axioms and rules of inference. In the end, everybody must understand for himself.
          %p Moreover, as mentioned above, the meaning explanation (like any particular semantics) validates many other “rules” which are not usually added to type theories, such as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi Id
                    %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mn 5
                  %mo :
                  %mi N
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : Id_N(0,1)  \vdash  5 : N \to N
          %p
            (However in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nuprl"} Nuprl
            rules such as these are, in fact, included.) Thus, some discrimination is required, as well as some choices (e.g. whether to add the extensionality rule for identity types).
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-L%C3%B6f"}> Per Martin-Löf
                ,
                %em Intuitionistic Type Theory
                (Notes by Giovanni Sambin of a series of lectures given in Padua, June 1980). Napoli, Bibliopolis, 1984. (
                %a{:href => "http://archive-pml.github.io/martin-lof/pdfs/Bibliopolis-Book-retypeset-1984.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-L%C3%B6f"}> Per Martin-Löf
                ,
                %em Constructive mathematics and computer programming
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.tufts.edu/~nr/cs257/archive/per-martin-lof/constructive-math.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-L%C3%B6f"}> Per Martin-Löf
                \.
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the Meanings of the Logical Constants and the Justifications of the Logical Laws
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://docenti.lett.unisi.it/files/4/1/1/6/martinlof4.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-L%C3%B6f"}> Per Martin-Löf
                \.
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Philosophical Aspects of Intuitionistic Type Theory
              %p
                of a lecture course given at Leiden University in the autumn semester of 1993. (
                %a{:href => "https://pml.flu.cas.cz/uploads/PML-LeidenLectures93.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UF-IAS-2012"}> UF-IAS-2012
                ,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/ufias2012/published/Meaning%20Explanations"} Meaning Explanations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Sterling"}> Jonathan Sterling
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Type Theory and its Meaning Explanations
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.01837"} arxiv
          %p
            Proposal for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %ul
            %li#Tsementzis17
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dimitris+Tsementzis"}> Dimitris Tsementzis
              ,
              %em A Meaning Explanation for HoTT
              (
              %a{:href => "http://philsci-archive.pitt.edu/12824/"}> philsci-archive.pitt.edu/12824
              ))
